    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make an issue of the panel being all men**, nor is it likely a meaningful factor in why you were not selected. \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of what that panel composition means, why the decision likely went the way it did, and how to proceed:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does the all-male panel suggest bias against you?\nFrom a fairness or legal perspective, almost certainly not:\n* **Both you and the selected candidate are men.** An all-male panel selecting one man over another does not indicate gender discrimination.\n* Raising the gender composition of the panel would likely appear adversarial, out of touch, or grasp at straws, which could damage your professional reputation in a regional municipal network.\n\n### 2. Is it a sign of anything broader?\nWhile it doesn’t explain the hiring decision between two male candidates, it can indicate a few things about the municipality:\n* **Senior leadership demographics:** In many mid-sized municipalities, selection panels for director-level roles typically consist of the Chief Administrative Officer (CAO), a peer director (e.g., Public Works or Engineering), an HR representative, and sometimes an external advisor or council liaison. An all-male panel usually just mirrors who currently occupies those specific executive seats.\n* **Organizational modernization:** Progressive public organizations often strive for balanced panels today. A lack of diversity may suggest a traditional, slow-to-change corporate culture, but it does not inherently imply malice or procedural unfairness.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What likely drove the hiring decision?\nComparing your background to the public announcement gives strong clues about what the panel prioritized:\n\n| Factor | Your Profile | Successful Candidate |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Education & Credentials** | Master’s in Urban Planning, professional licence | Bachelor’s in Geography |\n| **Experience Emphasis** | 12 years municipal planning experience | \"Extensive experience of public consultation\" |\n\nAt the **Director** level in municipal administration, the job changes fundamentally from technical planning to **political and stakeholder management**:\n* **Council & Community Relations:** Directors spend much of their time presenting to city council, fielding controversy at statutory public meetings, dealing with developers, and managing community outrage (NIMBYism). \n* **The \"Public Consultation\" clue:** The municipality specifically highlighting the winner's consultation experience suggests this municipality may currently be facing contentious growth issues, major master plan updates, or difficult public engagement challenges where they felt strong communication and political acumen outweighed a technical master's degree or licence.\n* **Soft factors & interview dynamic:** At the shortlist stage (top 3 after two rounds), all candidates are presumed capable. Decisions at that point almost always hinge on interview rapport, perceived leadership presence, communication style, and alignment with the CAO’s vision.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Why haven't they responded after two weeks?\nA two-week silence following a request for written feedback is very standard in municipal government:\n* Written feedback often requires HR and legal vetting to avoid creating liabilities.\n* The formal offer and background check process for the successful candidate may still be finalizing.\n* Municipal HR processes move notoriously slowly.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Let go of the panel composition angle.** Focusing on it will not provide useful closure or actionable insight.\n2. **Follow up gently on feedback:**\n   * Send a polite, constructive email to your HR contact:\n     > *\"Thank you again for the opportunity to interview for the Director of Planning position. I understand your team is busy finalizing the recruitment, but I would greatly appreciate 10–15 minutes by phone or a few brief written pointers on areas where I can strengthen my candidacy for future executive roles.\"*\n   * A phone debrief is often much easier to get than a formal written debrief, as hiring managers are less concerned about legal exposure over a quick call.\n3. **Focus the feedback inquiry on executive competencies:** When you do speak with them, ask specifically whether there were particular leadership, political, or public engagement skill sets they felt tipped the scale.\n",
